WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.540
Going to the movies isn't cheap these days,
but sometimes, finishing a flick just isn't

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.870
worth it, or even possible.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.330
Movies have the power to move us in many ways,
including out of our seats and straight to

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.820
the EXIT signs.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.150
The following films forced audience members
to bail before the credits rolled, thanks

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:24.599
to disturbing content, nauseating cinematography,
and, in one case, utter boredom.

00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:26.380
The House That Jack Built

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.369
Danish director Lars von Trier is known for
making dark, upsetting arthouse fare that's

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:34.140
celebrated and denounced in equal measure.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.960
He's also known for causing a stir at the
Cannes Film Festival.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.309
AFP reported that "at least four people fainted"
in 2009 during the Cannes screening of his

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.269
experimental horror film Antichrist.

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:49.239
But his 2018 showing at Cannes was his most
controversial yet.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.200
"Help!

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.220
There's a murderer in the building!

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.820
It's Mr. Sophistication!"

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.500
The Independent reported more than 100 walkouts
in the middle of The House That Jack Built,

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.700
which stars Matt Dillon as a child-mutilating
serial killer.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.930
One disgusted moviegoer told Deadline,

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.030
"[It was like] spending two hours in Hell."

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.770
The film still received a standing ovation
from those left in attendance, with one Guardian

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:21.729
critic raving that the finale, in particular,
was, quote, "spectacular."

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:22.729
Cloverfield

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:27.750
The 2008 found-footage horror film Cloverfield
follows six Manhattan millennials attending

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.240
a party when a gigantic monster attacks the
city.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.509
Their experience is shakily documented on
a personal video camera later recovered by

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:37.670
the Department of Defense.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:41.240
Audience members reportedly bailed left and
right during Cloverfield's record-breaking

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.110
opening weekend, complaining of migraines,
nausea, and motion sickness caused by the

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.670
film's unsettling cinematography.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.289
"I don't feel so good."

00:01:50.289 --> 00:01:55.810
Some theaters even posted warning signs comparing
watching the film to riding a rollercoaster.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.799
Inherent Vice

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:02.149
Director Paul Thomas Anderson has amassed
a considerable amount of cred with critics

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:07.110
and casual moviegoers alike with films such
as Boogie Nights and There Will Be Blood.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:13.860
But his star-studded 2014 adaptation of Thomas
Pynchon's '70s noir novel Inherent Vice caused

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.000
enough audience walkouts for the media to
take notice, thanks to the power of Twitter.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.150
Steve Rose of the Guardian wrote about the
online reaction in a piece subtitled "How

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.230
to make a film your audience will be dying
to leave."

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:32.000
Rose gathered several "walk out" tweets from
prominent UK writers baffled by the Joaquin

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.810
Phoenix-led flick, including one from novelist
Philip Hensher, who tweeted, "Understood so

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.230
little of plot or dialogue, I worried I’d
had a stroke."

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.709
But it wasn't an isolated phenomenon: Rose
reported that the internet was "buzzing" with

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:47.770
similar reports, including:

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.180
"Saw Inherent Vice and at least a quarter
of the audience walked out"

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.310
"I couldn’t sit through more than an hour
of Inherent Vice"

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.310
and

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.459
"50% walk out rate is highest I’ve seen
in awhile”

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:03.989
Many viewers found the dark comedy incomprehensible
and boring, but a lot of critics were impressed

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:09.190
with the movie, calling it a faithful adaptation
of the equally puzzling source material.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:13.569
If you're willing to put in the time, Jordan
Hoffman from Vanity Fair advises it's far

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:16.450
more rewarding if you watch it twice.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:17.489
The Revenant

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:22.920
Leonardo DiCaprio finally won his first Academy
Award for his starring role in the brutal

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.810
wilderness revenge epic The Revenant.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:31.110
But when the film was first screened for awards
voters in November 2015, many Academy members

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.659
in attendance reportedly couldn't handle the
movie's more gruesome moments, including the

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:37.840
now-famous bear attack.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.879
Scott Feinberg of the Hollywood Reporter noted,

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:44.719
"... several people walked out throughout
the movie, apparently unable to take the gore

00:03:44.719 --> 00:03:45.719
…"

00:03:45.719 --> 00:03:49.959
Film historian and critic Leonard Maltin later
expressed doubts about the film's chances

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:51.720
for Oscar glory, explaining,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.230
"The older members of the Academy will accept
a certain amount of violence if the balance

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.060
of the film pleases them.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.620
But if, as they leave the theater, the lingering
thought is how violent it was, that can be

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:03.829
a detriment."

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:08.340
In the case of The Revenant, several Academy
members left the theater early because of

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.939
the violence, but director Alejandro G. Iñárritu
still won his fourth Oscar, and the film was

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:17.970
nominated in 12 categories, the most of any
film that year.

